<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Gerrha</h1><p class="st-meta">400 BCE – 100 CE (approx.) · capital Gerrha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A legendary trading city of eastern Arabia, described by Greek writers as the richest place in the world for its incense and spice trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gerrha is known mainly from Greek and Roman writers, who say it was founded by Chaldean exiles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Gulf</a> coast of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. It flourished from around the 4th century BCE. Its site has never been identified with certainty; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaj">Thaj</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hofuf">Hofuf</a> and the coast near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqair">Uqair</a> have all been proposed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Gerrhaeans carried frankincense and other goods from south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> across the desert and shipped Indian wares up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Gulf</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agatharchides">Agatharchides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describe houses decorated with gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. In 205 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> sailed to Gerrha and accepted a tribute of silver, frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a> in return for leaving it free. Local coins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> legends were struck in the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Gerrha fades from the sources around the 1st century CE as Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> trade and the Nabataean and Palmyrene routes took over. Its precise location remains one of the open questions of Arabian archaeology.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gerrha.jpg" alt="Gerrha" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: MoeyElan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gerrha.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 BCE – 100 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gerrha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Arabian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Hasaitic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Gerrha, Gerrhaeans</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-150&amp;focus=gerrha" aria-label="Gerrha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6004 1224 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gerrha lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6324 1480L6140 1232"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1232h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6324 1480h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6324 1480h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gerrha. <a href="/atlas?year=-150&amp;focus=gerrha">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

